---
import CareerHighlight from "../components/CareerHighlight.astro";
import IconOpen from "../components/IconOpen.astro";
import Highlight from "../components/Highlight.astro";
import Logo from "../components/Logo.astro";
import ThemeSelector from "../components/ThemeSelector.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const roles = [
	{
		company: "Odaia",
		href: "https://www.odaia.ai",
		role: "Director, Product & Product Design",
		summary:
			"Built ODAIA’s Product Design function and later took on leadership of Product and Design. Managed three designers and three product managers while remaining hands-on in product strategy, research, AI product design, and AI first design systems.",
		tags: [
			"B2B Design",
			"Product & Design Leadership",
			"People Leadership",
			"AI Products",
			"AI First Design System",
			"AI Design Operations",
			"End-to-End Design",
		],
	},
	{
		company: "Loopio",
		href: "https://www.loopio.com",
		role: "Director of Product Design",
		summary:
			"Joined as Loopio’s first designer, owned the core product experience, and later hired and managed a team of three designers. Worked closely with the founders on product strategy while establishing the company’s design system, design practice, and cross-functional development model.",
		tags: [
			"B2B Design",
			"Design Leadership",
			"People Leadership",
			"Knowledge Systems",
			"Design Systems",
			"Enterprise SaaS",
			"Multi-Role UX",
			"End-to-End Design",
		],
	},
	{
		company: "Top Hat",
		href: "https://tophat.com",
		role: "Principle Product Designer",
		summary:
			"First and only designer through Series A, B, and C, responsible for the student and professor experience across web, mobile, SMS, iOS, and Android. Led product and platform design as Top Hat grew from a classroom response system into a broader education platform.",
		tags: [
			"B2B & B2C Design",
			"Platform Design",
			"EdTech",
			"Mobile Design",
			"Design Systems",
			"Front-End Developer",
			"Multi-Role UX",
			"End-to-End Design",
		],
	},
	{
		company: "Straight Line Phobia",
		role: "Product Design consulting",
		summary:
			"Worked with early-stage companies to turn emerging ideas and customer needs into coherent products. Often operated as the entire design function, shaping product direction and designing workflows from initial concept through launch.",
		tags: ["0-1 Product Design", "Product Strategy", "End-to-End Design"],
	},
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Ryan William Snow"
	description="Question everything. Care about the details."
>
	<div class="page">
		<header class="page-header">
			<ThemeSelector />
			<Logo />
		</header>

		<main class="main-content">
			<div class="home-main">
				<article class="essay">
					<header class="essay-header">
						<h1>Question everything. Care about the details.</h1>
						<p class="subhead">
							Sometimes the box you’re designing inside was built from constraints no one stopped
							to question.
						</p>
					</header>
					<div class="essay-body">
						<p>
							I’ve spent much of my career working on complex B2B products, and one pattern keeps
							showing up: products accumulate assumptions. A workflow designed for one customer
							becomes a convention. A technical limitation becomes a product rule. A configuration
							option becomes something everyone assumes users need. Over time, those decisions can
							harden into facts about the product. Teams learn to work around them rather than
							revisit whether they still reflect how people actually think and work.
						</p>
						<p>
							I’ve learned to make questioning the constraints around a problem part of how I work.
							Which constraints are real, and which are inherited assumptions we’ve stopped
							questioning? What is the user actually trying to accomplish? Does the system reflect
							how people really think and work? Are we solving the problem, or just making a
							symptom easier to live with? Some of my strongest work has come from stepping outside
							those constraints and realizing that the product model, the system, or our approach to
							the problem needed to change.
						</p>
						<p>
							Stepping back from the interface doesn’t make the details less important. If anything,
							it makes them more meaningful. I’m wary of words like craft and delight when they
							become shorthand for “make it nicer.” They matter when they’re doing something useful
							you can point to: reducing friction, building trust, making a system easier to learn,
							or helping someone act with more confidence.
						</p>
						<p>
							The details are where people feel that the product was made with them in mind. Someone
							thought about this, anticipated what they needed, and cared enough to get it right.
						</p>
					</div>
				</article>

				<aside class="panel">
					<section class="panel-block">
						<p class="name">Ryan<br />William<br />Snow</p>
						<p class="panel-copy">
							I’m a product design leader with 14+ years of experience building complex B2B SaaS and
							AI products. Much of my work involves turning complex data and system logic into
							products people can understand, trust, and act on. I’ve been a first design hire, built
							teams and design systems from scratch, and led both designers and product managers
							while staying hands-on in product strategy and design.
						</p>
						<img
							class="portrait"
							src={`${base}portrait.jpg`}
							width="326"
							height="518"
							alt="Ryan William Snow sitting at a cafe"
						/>
					</section>
				</aside>
			</div>

			<Highlight class="section-mark" />

			<div class="career-layout">
				<section class="career" aria-labelledby="career-heading">
					<header class="career-header">
						<h2 id="career-heading">Career Overview</h2>
						<nav class="link-row" aria-label="Career and contact">
							<a href="/cv">Check out my CV</a>
							<a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryanwsnow/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
								Reach me on Linkedin
							</a>
						</nav>
					</header>
					{roles.map((role) => <CareerHighlight {...role} />)}
				</section>

				<aside class="projects">
					<header class="projects-intro">
						<h2>Projects</h2>
						<p>Various side projects of note.</p>
					</header>
					<section class="project">
						<h3 class="project-title">
							<a href="https://missive.to" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Missive</a>
							<IconOpen />
						</h3>
						<p>
							Designed and built Missive with <a href="https://mackeracher.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Anson</a>, an experiment in slower, more intentional
							digital communication that borrows the rituals and anticipation of physical mail to
							encourage thoughtful correspondence between friends.
						</p>
					</section>
					<section class="project">
						<h3 class="project-title">AI-readable design system for Missive</h3>
						<p>
							Built an AI-readable design system for Missive, combining Figma, design tokens, React
							components, and Storybook with explicit implementation contracts so coding agents could
							work independently while preserving design consistency.
						</p>
					</section>
					<section class="project">
						<h3 class="project-title">
							<a href="https://metronome.ryanwsnow.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Metronome</a>
							<IconOpen />
						</h3>
						<p>An experiment to see how faithfully Cursor could translate a fairly complex Figma design into code.</p>
						<a href="https://metronome.ryanwsnow.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
							<img
								class="project-image"
								src={`${base}metronome.png`}
								width="430"
								height="932"
								alt="Metronome"
							/>
						</a>
					</section>
				</aside>
			</div>
		</main>

		<footer class="page-footer">
			<Highlight class="highlight" />
			<p>don’t worry, spiders · I keep house · casually</p>
		</footer>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>
